  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "Which triangle similarity criterion uses two angle pairs?" "AA similarity" "AA similarity uses two pairs of congruent corresponding angles." "SSS similarity" "SSS uses three side ratios." "SAS similarity" "SAS uses side ratios and an included angle." "CPCTC" "CPCTC is not a similarity criterion.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "Which triangle similarity criterion uses two side ratios and one included angle?" "SAS similarity" "SAS similarity matches two proportional sides with the included congruent angle." "AA similarity" "AA uses two angle pairs." "SSS similarity" "SSS uses all three side ratios." "ASA congruence" "ASA is a congruence criterion, not this similarity criterion.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "If &triangle;JKL &sim; &triangle;PQR, which side corresponds to KL?" "QR" "The statement maps J to P, K to Q, and L to R, so KL maps to QR." "PQ" "JK corresponds to PQ." "PR" "JL corresponds to PR." "JK" "JK is a side in the first triangle, not the corresponding side in the second.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "If &triangle;RST &sim; &triangle;XYZ, which side corresponds to RS?" "XY" "R maps to X and S maps to Y, so RS maps to XY." "YZ" "ST maps to YZ." "XZ" "RT maps to XZ." "ST" "ST is a different side in the first triangle.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "Two triangles have side ratios 1.2, 1.2, and 1.2. Which conclusion follows?" "The triangles are similar by SSS similarity." "All three corresponding side ratios are equal." "The triangles are congruent." "A ratio of 1.2 changes size, so congruence is not shown." "Only two sides are proportional." "All three listed ratios match." "The triangles are unrelated." "Equal ratios provide similarity evidence.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "Two triangles have two proportional side pairs but the angle given is not included. Can SAS similarity be used?" "No, the angle must be included." "SAS similarity requires the congruent angle between the proportional sides." "Yes, any angle works." "Position matters for SAS similarity." "Yes, because two side ratios are enough." "Two side ratios alone are not enough for SAS." "No, because side ratios never matter." "Side ratios matter, but the included angle condition is missing.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.3" "Which explanation is strongest?" "The triangles are similar by AA because two corresponding angle pairs are congruent." "This names both the evidence and the correct criterion." "The triangles look similar." "Appearance is not enough evidence." "The triangles are probably similar." "A proof needs specific evidence." "The triangles have labels." "Labels alone do not establish similarity."
)

# Lesson 4 proportional relationships
$l4 = @(
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Two similar figures have corresponding sides 5 and 20. What is the scale factor from first to second?" "4" "20 divided by 5 equals 4." "15" "This subtracts instead of comparing by ratio." "25" "This adds the side lengths." "&frac14;" "This is the reverse scale factor.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A similar triangle has scale factor 3. A side of 11 becomes what length?" "33" "Multiply 11 by 3." "14" "Adding 3 does not use scale factor." "8" "Subtracting 3 does not use scale factor." "3.67" "Dividing by 3 scales in reverse.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A map scale is 1 inch to 25 miles. How far is 4 inches?" "100 miles" "Four inches represents 4 times 25 miles." "29 miles" "Adding 4 and 25 does not use the scale." "6.25 miles" "Dividing 25 by 4 reverses the relationship." "75 miles" "That would match 3 inches, not 4.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A 5-ft person casts a 6-ft shadow. A tree casts an 18-ft shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the tree?" "15 ft" "The shadow scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; 5 times 3 is 15." "17 ft" "Adding 12 to the height copies a difference rather than a ratio." "21.6 ft" "Multiplying by 4.32 does not use the shadow scale factor." "10 ft" "This does not match the scale factor of 3.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Two congruent figures are similar with what scale factor?" "1" "Conguent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1." "0" "A scale factor of 0 collapses lengths." "2" "A scale factor of 2 changes size." "&frac12;" "A scale factor of &frac12; changes size.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A model car is 12 cm long at a 1:20 scale. What is the actual length?" "240 cm" "Actual length is 20 times the model length: 12 times 20." "32 cm" "Adding 20 does not use the scale ratio." "8 cm" "Subtracting 20 is not meaningful here." "0.6 cm" "Dividing by 20 scales in the wrong direction.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A drawing uses scale factor &frac15;. An actual length is 60 cm. What is the drawing length?" "12 cm" "60 times &frac15; equals 12." "55 cm" "Subtracting 5 does not use scale." "65 cm" "Adding 5 does not use scale." "300 cm" "This uses the reciprocal scale and scales up.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "If corresponding sides are 7 to 14 and 9 to x, what is x?" "18" "The scale factor is 2, so 9 becomes 18." "16" "Adding 7 repeats a difference rather than a scale." "11" "Adding 2 does not use the scale factor." "4.5" "Dividing by 2 scales in the wrong direction.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Which proportion matches corresponding sides 3 to 12 and 5 to x?" "<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> = <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>" "Corresponding sides must be placed in the same order in both ratios." "<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>" "This compares sides from different figures in the first ratio." "3 + 12 = 5 + x" "Similarity uses multiplicative ratios, not addition." "3x = 5" "This omits the corresponding side length 12.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A 2-inch drawing length represents 18 feet. How many feet does 5 inches represent?" "45 feet" "The scale is 9 feet per inch, so 5 inches represents 45 feet." "23 feet" "Adding 5 to 18 does not use the scale." "36 feet" "This would match 4 inches at 9 feet per inch." "7.2 feet" "Dividing 36 by 5 does not match the scale relationship.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A side of 16 corresponds to 10 in a smaller similar figure. What is the scale factor from larger to smaller?" "0.625" "The scale factor is 10 divided by 16, or 0.625." "6" "This subtracts side lengths." "26" "This adds side lengths." "1.6" "This is the reverse scale factor from smaller to larger.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A similar polygon has scale factor 2.5. A 4-unit side becomes what length?" "10 units" "4 times 2.5 equals 10." "6.5 units" "Adding 2.5 does not use scale factor." "1.6 units" "Dividing by 2.5 scales in reverse." "8 units" "This uses a scale factor of 2, not 2.5.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A 6-cm model represents 150 cm. What is the scale factor from model to actual?" "25" "150 divided by 6 equals 25." "144" "This subtracts lengths instead of finding the ratio." "156" "This adds lengths instead of finding the ratio." "0.04" "This is the reverse scale factor from actual to model.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A shadow problem creates similar triangles. A 4-ft pole casts an 8-ft shadow; a building casts a 40-ft shadow. What is the building height?" "20 ft" "The shadow scale factor is 5, so 4 times 5 is 20." "36 ft" "Subtracting or using the shadow difference does not preserve the ratio." "44 ft" "Adding 40 and 4 does not use similar triangles." "10 ft" "This uses only half the needed scale factor.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Two similar pentagons have scale factor 0.5 from P to Q. A side in P is 18. What is the corresponding side in Q?" "9" "18 times 0.5 equals 9." "18.5" "Adding 0.5 does not use scale." "36" "This uses the reciprocal and scales up." "17.5" "Subtracting 0.5 does not use scale.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "What information is needed to set up a proportion for similar figures?" "Corresponding side lengths" "Proportions compare matching corresponding sides." "Only the area color" "Color is unrelated to proportional side lengths." "Only the number of vertices" "The number of vertices does not determine side ratios." "Only the page size" "Page size does not provide corresponding lengths.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A scale factor from A to B is 4. A side in B is 52. What was the corresponding side in A?" "13" "Divide 52 by 4 to reverse the scale factor." "56" "Adding 4 does not reverse scale." "48" "Subtracting 4 does not reverse scale." "208" "Multiplying by 4 scales from A to B again.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A similar rectangle has width 30 corresponding to original width 12. What is the scale factor?" "2.5" "30 divided by 12 equals 2.5." "18" "This subtracts widths." "42" "This adds widths." "0.4" "This is the reverse scale factor.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A blueprint has a 3-inch wall representing 24 feet. What does 7 inches represent?" "56 feet" "The scale is 8 feet per inch, so 7 inches represents 56 feet." "31 feet" "Adding 7 and 24 does not use the scale." "72 feet" "This would match 9 inches at the same scale." "3.43 feet" "Dividing 24 by 7 does not use the unit rate correctly.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "If x corresponds to 24 and 5 corresponds to 15, what is x?" "8" "The scale factor is 3, so x times 3 equals 24; x is 8." "12" "This uses an incorrect scale factor." "34" "Adding the known values is not proportional reasoning." "72" "This multiplies 24 by 3 instead of reversing the scale.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A scale drawing is 9 cm tall and the actual object is 36 cm tall. What is the scale factor from drawing to actual?" "4" "36 divided by 9 equals 4." "27" "This subtracts heights." "45" "This adds heights." "0.25" "This is the reverse scale factor from actual to drawing.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Two similar triangles have scale factor &frac34; from large to small. A large side is 20. What is the small side?" "15" "20 times &frac34; equals 15." "23" "Adding 3 does not use the scale factor." "80" "Multiplying by 4 does not use &frac34;." "26.67" "Dividing by &frac34; scales in the opposite direction.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A student sets 4 + 6 = 10 + x for similar figures. What is the misconception?" "Using addition instead of ratios" "Similar figures are solved with proportional ratios, not additive sums." "Using corresponding sides" "Using corresponding sides is correct; the issue is the operation." "Using too many variables" "A variable may be used for an unknown side." "Checking scale factor" "Checking scale factor is part of the correct method.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "Which statement is true for congruent two-dimensional figures?" "Corresponding sides are equal." "Congruent figures have matching side lengths and angle measures." "Corresponding sides are always doubled." "Doubling describes a similar figure with scale factor 2, not congruence." "Angles are unrelated." "Congruent figures have matching corresponding angles." "Scale factor cannot be found." "The scale factor is 1 for congruent figures.",
  T "MLA.GEO.SIM.1" "A side length changes from 2.4 to 12 in a similar figure. What scale factor was used?" "5" "12 divided by 2.4 equals 5." "9.6" "This subtracts side lengths." "14.4" "This adds side lengths." "0.2" "This is the reverse scale factor."
)

# Lesson 5 Pythagorean readiness
$l5 = @()
$py = @(@(3,4,5),@(5,12,13),@(8,15,17),@(7,24,25),@(9,40,41),@(12,16,20),@(10,24,26),@(20,21,29),@(11,60,61),@(28,45,53))
foreach($t in $py){ $a=$t[0]; $b=$t[1]; $c=$t[2]; $l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "A right triangle has legs $a and $b. What is the hypotenuse?" "$c" "Use a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>; the square root of the sum is $c." "$($a+$b)" "Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse." "$([math]::Abs($b-$a))" "Subtracting the legs does not use the Pythagorean Theorem." "$($a*$b)" "Multiplying the legs does not find the hypotenuse." }
foreach($t in $py){ $a=$t[0]; $b=$t[1]; $c=$t[2]; $l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "A right triangle has hypotenuse $c and one leg $a. What is the other leg?" "$b" "Use b<sup>2</sup> = $c<sup>2</sup> - $a<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is $b." "$($c-$a)" "Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship." "$($c+$a)" "Adding the known lengths does not find a missing leg." "&radic;$($c*$c+$a*$a)" "Adding squares would be used if both known sides were legs, but $c is the hypotenuse." }
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "Which side is opposite the right angle?" "The hypotenuse" "The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle." "A leg" "The legs form the right angle." "The shorter side" "The shorter side is usually a leg, not the side opposite the right angle." "The adjacent side" "Adjacent depends on a chosen acute angle."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "Which equation fits legs 14 and 48 with hypotenuse c?" "14<sup>2</sup> + 48<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>" "The squares of the legs add to the square of the hypotenuse." "14 + 48 = c<sup>2</sup>" "The legs must be squared before adding." "14c + 48 = 0" "This does not represent the Pythagorean relationship." "14<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> = 48<sup>2</sup>" "This treats 48 as the hypotenuse instead of c."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "Which set of side lengths forms a right triangle?" "6, 8, 10" "6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup>." "4, 5, 6" "4<sup>2</sup> + 5<sup>2</sup> is not 6<sup>2</sup>." "5, 6, 7" "5<sup>2</sup> + 6<sup>2</sup> is not 7<sup>2</sup>." "6, 7, 12" "6<sup>2</sup> + 7<sup>2</sup> is not 12<sup>2</sup>."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "What is &radic;144?" "12" "12 squared equals 144." "72" "Dividing by 2 does not find a square root." "24" "Doubling 12 is not the square root." "&radic;12" "&radic;144 simplifies completely to 12."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "What is the longest side of a right triangle called?" "Hypotenuse" "The hypotenuse is the longest side and is opposite the right angle." "Leg" "Legs form the right angle and are shorter than the hypotenuse." "Median" "A median is a segment from a vertex to a midpoint." "Altitude" "An altitude is a height segment."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "A triangle has sides 9, 12, and 15. Why is it right?" "9<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> = 15<sup>2</sup>" "The two smaller side squares sum to the largest side square." "9 + 12 = 15" "9 + 12 equals 21, and addition is not the right-triangle test." "15 - 12 = 9" "15 - 12 equals 3, and subtraction is not the test." "9(12) = 15" "Multiplication of side lengths is not the Pythagorean test."
$l5 += T "Readiness Support Only" "If c is the hypotenuse, what equation is the Pythagorean Theorem?" "a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>" "The sum of the squares of the legs equals the square of the hypotenuse." "a + b = c" "Side lengths do not add directly in the theorem." "a<sup>2</sup> - b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>" "The theorem uses a sum of leg squares." "ab = c<sup>2</sup>" "The theorem uses squares, not the product of legs."

